## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

### Summary

**10 tests analyzed · 8 Critical · 1 High · 1 Medium · 0 Low**

This suite is a textbook **coverage-touching** test class. Every public method on `ProductCatalog` is called — producing 95% line coverage — but almost nothing is actually verified. A bug in any of these methods would not cause a test failure.

---

### 🔴 Critical — Tests Giving False Confidence

#### 1. Coverage-Touching Pattern (Systemic)

The entire suite methodically calls every public method in declaration order with no meaningful assertions. 5 of 10 tests have **zero assertions**; the other 5 use only `Assert.IsNotNull`, which proves a reference was returned but not that it's *correct*.

#### 2. No Assertions (5 tests)

| Test | Line | What It Misses |
|---|---|---|
| `AddProduct_Works` | 17–21 | Never verifies the product was actually stored |
| `UpdatePrice_Works` | 32–36 | Never checks that the price changed to 14.99 |
| `RemoveProduct_Works` | 38–43 | Never checks that the product is gone |
| `GetProductCount_Works` | 63–66 | Calls `GetProductCount()` and discards the result |
| `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` | 68–74 | Never checks that prices were reduced by 10% |
| `ImportFromCsv_Works` | 84–88 | Never checks that any product was imported |

**Fix examples:**

```csharp
// AddProduct_Works → AddProduct_StoresProduct
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_StoresProduct()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);

    var stored = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual("Widget", stored?.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, stored?.Price);
}

// UpdatePrice_Works → UpdatePrice_ChangesPrice
[TestMethod]
public void UpdatePrice_ChangesPrice()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);

    var updated = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, updated?.Price);
}

// RemoveProduct_Works → RemoveProduct_DeletesProduct
[TestMethod]
public void RemoveProduct_DeletesProduct()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");

    Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
}

// GetProductCount_Works → GetProductCount_ReturnsCorrectCount
[TestMethod]
public void GetProductCount_ReturnsCorrectCount()
{
    Assert.AreEqual(0, _catalog.GetProductCount());
